# One fingerprint, pinned to one commit, one file, one line.
#
# 95c2953a bridge/scripts/acceptance-unpair.sh:112 is the string
# `Bearer tok-never-issued-by-anyone` — the deliberately invalid token in the
# assertion that an unknown token answers `invalid_token` rather than
# `token_revoked`. It authenticates nothing; the bridge has never issued it.
#
# Verified two ways on 2026-08-04, independently by two people: every real
# token in that script reaches curl through $TOKEN (sole assignment at :75,
# read from the pairing response), and the flagged value is the only literal
# among the four Bearer headers in the file.
#
# The rule stays on. `curl-auth-header` matches the shape `Bearer <literal>`
# and never inspects the value, so it cannot tell a fake from a credential —
# which is exactly why it must keep firing everywhere else. This exempts one
# fingerprint, not the rule. Positive control, run before committing this
# file: a realistic token planted in a new script under bridge/scripts/ was
# still reported (`leaks found: 1`), and removing it returned the tree to
# `no leaks found`.
#
# Why an ignore entry rather than only editing the script: `gitleaks detect`
# scans history, and the finding is anchored to a commit that already exists.
# Editing the working tree cannot clear a blob that is already reachable, so
# the job stays red on every branch regardless of what the file says today.
# The alternative was rewriting published history, which costs every open
# branch a rebase to erase a string that was never a secret.
#
# The script itself is fixed separately on `feat/bridge-skeleton`, where the
# value is assembled through a variable so no *new* finding can appear. That
# branch is not merged; this entry covers the history that exists today, and
# is what unblocks the five pull requests currently failing on it.
